Objective

Understand how HTTPS traffic can be decrypted in Wireshark when TLS session keys are available.

Tools Used

Wireshark
Chromium
TLS Key Log File

Steps Performed

Opened randy-chromium.pcapng
Configured Wireshark:
Edit → Preferences
Protocols → TLS
Added ssl-key.log
Reloaded the capture
TLS packets were decrypted into HTTP/2 traffic
Searched for /login
Located the HTTP/2 request
Examined decrypted headers
Retrieved the required information

Key Concepts Learned

HTTPS traffic is normally encrypted.
TLS session keys can be used to decrypt captured traffic.
Wireshark supports TLS decryption through SSL Key Log Files.
HTTP/2 headers become visible after successful decryption.
Security analysts often inspect decrypted traffic during incident investigations.

Challenges Faced

Wireshark initially displayed a blank screen after loading the key log.
Resolved by reopening/reloading the capture.

Skills Demonstrated

Packet Analysis
TLS Decryption
Wireshark Usage
HTTP/2 Traffic Analysis
Incident Investigation Fundamentals

